I've completed my thorough independent verification. Let me compile my assessment.

```json
{
  "verdict": "revise",
  "scores": {
    "novelty": {
      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "The specific niche of role-aware extractive compression (bridge-entity carrier vs. supporting premise vs. filler) is genuinely unoccupied — no existing work does this. However, the technical contribution is incremental: it extends EXIT's binary classification to 3-class classification with heuristic label derivation from existing HotpotQA annotations. The label derivation pipeline (spaCy NER + paragraph overlap) is straightforward engineering, not a methodological innovation. CUE-R (arXiv 2604.05467, April 2026) already proposes an evidence-role taxonomy for RAG evaluation on the same benchmarks, partially eroding the novelty of reasoning-role distinction."
    },
    "feasibility": {
      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "The resource estimate (120-170h H100) fits within the 200h constraint, but the venue deadline is critically wrong: EMNLP 2026 ARR is May 25, 2026 (11 days), NOT June 15 — the June cycle cannot be committed to EMNLP 2026 per the CFP. The manual timeline estimate (4-5 weeks) vastly exceeds the 11-day constraint. Bridge entity identification via spaCy NER is a real accuracy risk: not all bridge entities are named entities (concepts, dates, events are missed), and the 85-90% agreement estimate is optimistic without pilot data."
    },
    "clarity": {
      "score": 4,
      "rationale": "The revised idea is well-structured with a clear hypothesis (role-aware helps specifically at extreme compression ratios), well-specified pipeline components (spaCy NER, heuristic rules, LoRA training), and focused experimental design (compression ratio sweep + ablation). The annotation pipeline is more concrete than round 1. The extreme-compression argument is explicit and testable."
    },
    "impact": {
      "score": 3,
      "rationale": "The improvement is limited by the authors' own hypothesis to extreme compression ratios (16-32x) where binary classification must discard relevant sentences. At moderate compression (≤4x) — the common operating regime — the method offers no benefit. The approach is tailored to HotpotQA's annotation structure (bridge questions with two gold paragraphs), and generalization to other datasets (2WikiMultiHopQA, MuSiQue) requires separate heuristics that are underspecified. This is a Findings-level contribution, not main conference."
    }
  },
  "issues": [
    "CRITICAL: Venue deadline is wrong. The generator claims 'ARR June 15, 2026 — 32 days' but the EMNLP 2026 CFP explicitly states the ARR submission deadline is May 25, 2026, and 'Papers must be submitted, at latest, by the ARR 2026 May cycle.' The June cycle CANNOT be committed to EMNLP 2026. This means only 11 days remain, not 32. The manual timeline estimate (4-5 weeks) is incompatible with this deadline.",
    "Bridge entity identification accuracy is under-validated. The 85-90% agreement claim has no empirical backing. SpaCy NER (en_core_web_lg) recognizes standard entity types (PERSON, ORG, GPE, etc.) but many HotpotQA bridge entities are concepts, events, or common nouns that fall outside NER coverage. Furthermore, when multiple named entities are shared between paragraphs, disambiguating which is the true bridge entity adds error. A pilot study on 200-500 examples should be conducted BEFORE committing to this approach.",
    "Missing recent related work: CUE-R (arXiv 2604.05467, April 2026) proposes an operational evidence-role taxonomy for RAG using intervention-based analysis on HotpotQA and 2WikiMultiHopQA. It directly addresses evidence functional roles in multi-hop RAG and must be discussed. Additionally, IndexRAG (arXiv 2603.16415) identifies bridge entities at index time for cross-document reasoning — a complementary approach that validates the bridge-entity concept but should be positioned against.",
    "BRIEF-Pro venue is misattributed as arXiv only — it has been accepted at ACL-Findings 2026. This should be corrected to properly position against a peer-reviewed baseline.",
    "The contribution is incremental: the entire technical novelty reduces to (1) a heuristic rule that identifies which supporting-fact sentence contains the bridge entity, and (2) replacing EXIT's binary cross-entropy with weighted multi-class cross-entropy. The LoRA training, Gemma-2B architecture, and evaluation setup are all inherited from EXIT. Top-venue reviewers may view this as an experiment on EXIT rather than a new method.",
    "Generalization to 2WikiMultiHopQA is underspecified. The role label derivation pipeline is tightly coupled to HotpotQA's annotation structure (gold supporting facts + bridge/comparison question types + two gold paragraphs). 2WikiMultiHopQA has different annotation conventions, and the generator does not specify how role labels will be derived for this dataset. Without this, the 'generalization test' claim is hollow.",
    "The extreme-compression regime (16-32x) where this method claims advantage is not well-motivated practically. EXIT operates at ~3.5x compression (28.3% retention) by default. BRIEF-Pro achieves 32x compression abstractively with 49.0 EM. Who needs 32x extractive compression when abstractive methods already handle it? The generator should articulate a concrete use case for extreme extractive compression."
  ],
  "feedback": "The core idea has genuine merit — reasoning-role-aware compression is a real gap — but the paper needs strengthening on multiple fronts to reach top-venue quality:\n\n1. **Fix the venue deadline immediately**: EMNLP 2026 ARR is May 25, not June 15. If May 25 is infeasible, honestly target ARR June (for AAAI 2027 or another venue) and adjust the timeline accordingly. Don't misstate the deadline.\n\n2. **Run a pilot study on bridge entity identification**: Before committing to the full pipeline, manually annotate 200-500 HotpotQA bridge questions and measure spaCy NER's bridge-entity identification accuracy. If it's below 75%, consider fallback strategies: (a) use LLM-based entity extraction instead of spaCy, (b) use string matching on paragraph titles (HotpotQA paragraphs are titled by their subject entity, making bridge identification trivial via title matching). Title-based bridge identification could be much more reliable than NER-based extraction.\n\n3. **Strengthen the contribution beyond heuristic labels**: The current technical novelty is thin. Consider: (a) a learnable role classifier that goes beyond heuristic rules (e.g., train on the 5K DeepSeek-R1 validated examples and generalize), (b) a curriculum training strategy where Gemma-2B first learns binary classification then refines to role-aware, (c) analysis showing that bridge-entity carriers have distinct embedding or attention patterns that justify role-aware selection.\n\n4. **Add CUE-R and IndexRAG to related work**: CUE-R's evidence-role taxonomy validates the intuition behind role-aware treatment. IndexRAG's bridge-entity identification validates the concept. Position ReasonComp as bringing role awareness into the compression pipeline specifically.\n\n5. **Motivate extreme extractive compression**: Explain why extractive 16-32x compression is desirable when abstractive compression (BRIEF-Pro) already handles it. Key arguments: (a) extractive preserves source fidelity for attribution, (b) extractive is faster (parallel classification vs. sequential generation), (c) extractive enables verifiable provenance — each token maps to a source document. Make this argument explicit.\n\n6. **Specify 2WikiMultiHopQA role label derivation**: Either detail how bridge entities will be identified in 2WikiMultiHopQA (it has different annotation structure) or honestly present it as a transfer/zero-shot evaluation using the HotpotQA-trained model without dataset-specific role labels.",
  "reason": "Genuine gap but incremental contribution (EXIT + one more label class), wrong venue deadline (May 25 not June 15), and under-validated annotation pipeline accuracy make this below top-venue threshold."
}
```
